Update the Deployment Manager UI with the following refinement to the Pod/container presentation.

1. POD STATUS

Keep the existing Pod replica/readiness display exactly as the primary indicator.

Examples:

Pods 1/1 Ready
Pods 2/2 Ready
Pods 1/2 Ready

The replica count must always represent Pods/replicas, never the number of containers inside those Pods.

2. CONDITIONAL CONTAINER BADGE

For deployments where a Pod contains multiple applicable containers, show the container count as a compact badge/chip on the SAME LINE as the Pods count.

Example:

Pods 1/1 Ready    Containers 2

The "Containers 2" indicator should use the same visual badge/chip style used elsewhere in the UI for statuses and metadata.

Do NOT create a new line just to display the container count.

Do NOT show "Containers 1" for normal single-container deployments.

Only display the container-count badge when there are multiple containers that are relevant to the workload, such as when a sidecar is present.

For normal single-container deployments, keep the UI exactly as it is:

Pods 1/1 Ready

This avoids redundant information and keeps the deployment list compact.

3. MULTI-CONTAINER SEMANTICS

Make sure the container count never affects the Pod/replica count.

For example, if one Pod contains:
- 1 application container
- 1 sidecar container

the Deployment Manager must still display:

Pods 1/1 Ready    Containers 2

NOT:

Pods 2/2 Ready

Similarly, if there are two Pods and each Pod contains multiple containers, the replica indicator must still represent the number of Pods:

Pods 2/2 Ready    Containers 2

The exact container badge value should represent the applicable container structure according to the existing backend/Kubernetes data model.

4. POD DETAIL VIEW

On the detailed Deployment page, keep the Pod-level Running/Ready status as the primary status.

Do not unnecessarily repeat "Running" at both the Pod level and every healthy container level.

Only show a container-level status when it provides meaningful information, such as:
- Not Ready
- CrashLoopBackOff
- Restarting
- Terminated
- Waiting
- Other non-healthy states

5. CONTAINER INFORMATION

When multiple containers are present, clearly group the individual containers within the Pod.

Keep:
- Container name
- Image
- Started time
- CPU
- Memory
- Relevant health/readiness information

Remove the image digest from the primary UI presentation.

Do not remove the digest from the backend data if it is currently being collected.

6. SELECTOR LABELS

Change the Deployment Overview section from "SELECTOR" to "SELECTOR LABELS".

Display each selector as an individual compact badge/chip, similar to the existing status badge styling.

For example:

SELECTOR LABELS

[app=...]
[deploymentconfig=...]
[logging=...]

Do not display the selector as one long unformatted string.

7. IMPORTANT UI RULE

Do not add unnecessary information to deployments that do not need it.

Normal single-container deployment:

Pods 1/1 Ready

Multi-container deployment:

Pods 1/1 Ready    Containers 2

Keep the additional container information conditional, compact, and on the same line.

Do not alter or remove any existing deployment functionality, actions, conditions, rollout history, events, CPU/memory information, or other existing data.

Preserve the current dark/neon glass-card design and visual language.
